 "licence_note": null,
 "history": "Under the 1751 regimental coat the private wore a long waistcoat, red for most regiments (the facing colour for a few), its skirts reaching the thigh below the coat's turned-back fronts (Morier's grenadier paintings of 1751) [S17][S4]. The summer 1758 orders cut waistcoats short; Lisle's men at the German Flatts that winter still had the long ones. Gentlemen's waistcoats of the 1750s were as long, of cloth or silk, often buff or matching the coat (the V&A's 1750s suit) [S12].",
